      "cultural_notes_vi": "Văn hóa peer review Hàn academia: (1) Tạp chí KCI có 3 mức quyết định: 게재 (accept), 수정 후 게재 (accept after revision), 게재 불가 (reject). 'Major revision' thực chất là 'conditional accept' nếu đáp ứng đủ. (2) Hệ thống thường blind — cả author và reviewer ẩn danh. KHÔNG đề tên người trong response letter. (3) Response letter ngày càng được đính kèm như supplementary material — public sau publication. Viết professional. (4) Hàn coi 'response letter perfunctory' (thư phản hồi qua loa) là dấu hiệu thiếu cẩn trọng — gây ấn tượng xấu cho editor lần sau. (5) Khi reviewer comments có vẻ không hiểu bài: KHÔNG argue 'reviewer didn't read carefully'. Soft: '본 부분의 표현이 명확하지 않았던 것으로 사료됩니다. 다음과 같이 보완하였습니다.' (6) Đối thoại giữa author và editor (편집장) qua email rất formal — bắt đầu bằng '편집장님께' (Dear Editor) và đóng bằng '깊이 감사드립니다.' (7) Khi reject quyết liệt nhưng bạn nghĩ unfair: có thể appeal qua editor-in-chief, nhưng rất hiếm thành công và damage reputation. Better: revise và submit tạp chí khác. (8) Việt vs Hàn: tone của response Việt thường thẳng hơn — sinh viên Việt nên thêm 1-2 lớp polite framing. (9) Reviewing cho người khác: nhận lời reviewing là nghĩa vụ học thuật — bạn có thời gian thì làm, không nên nhận quá 5 bài/năm.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Korean peer-review culture in academia: (1) KCI journals have 3 decisions: 게재 (accept), 수정 후 게재 (accept after revision), 게재 불가 (reject). 'Major revision' is effectively a 'conditional accept' if you meet the conditions. (2) The system is usually blind — both author and reviewer anonymous. Do NOT name anyone in the response letter. (3) The response letter is increasingly attached as supplementary material — public after publication. Write it professionally. (4) Korea reads a 'perfunctory response letter' as a sign of carelessness — it gives the editor a bad impression for next time. (5) When reviewer comments seem to misread the paper: do NOT argue 'the reviewer didn't read carefully'. Soft: '본 부분의 표현이 명확하지 않았던 것으로 사료됩니다. 다음과 같이 보완하였습니다.' (this part's wording was likely unclear; I've revised it as follows). (6) Author–editor (편집장) email is very formal — open with '편집장님께' (Dear Editor) and close with '깊이 감사드립니다.' (7) When a rejection is firm but you think it's unfair: you can appeal via the editor-in-chief, but it rarely succeeds and damages your reputation. Better: revise and submit to another journal. (8) Vietnamese vs Korean: a Vietnamese response tone is usually more direct — Vietnamese students should add 1–2 layers of polite framing. (9) Reviewing for others: accepting review requests is an academic duty — do it when you have time, but don't take more than ~5 papers/year.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Bộ khung response letter 4 phần: (1) Cover paragraph cảm ơn editor + reviewer; (2) Reviewer 1 block — quote, response, change; (3) Reviewer 2 block — same; (4) Closing paragraph hy vọng. Mẹo handling comments: phân loại trước. (a) ACCEPT — agree, can implement → response 'Done, see page X'; (b) PARTIAL ACCEPT — agree principle, can't fully implement → response 'I have addressed this by Y, but full implementation requires Z which is beyond current scope'; (c) RESPECTFUL DISAGREE — bạn nghĩ reviewer sai → response với evidence; (d) CLARIFICATION — reviewer hiểu lầm → response giải thích, sửa wording trong bài để clearer. Mẹo viết response cho disagree: NEVER 'reviewer is wrong'. ALWAYS 'In the context of this study, X' / 'A different interpretation may be possible'. Mẹo numbers: nếu có 12 comments, response letter ~3-5 trang là vừa. Quá ngắn = perfunctory; quá dài = bị editor ghét. Khi nộp lại: subject line '[Manuscript ID] Revised submission with response to reviewers'. Email gửi editor đính kèm: (a) revised manuscript clean version; (b) revised manuscript track changes; (c) response letter PDF. Cuối cùng: peer review là cuộc đối thoại học thuật — không phải combat. Reviewer cố giúp bài tốt hơn (kể cả khi tone gay gắt). Cảm ơn họ chân thành. Career-long: bạn sẽ là reviewer của ai đó — practice empathy from this side.",
      "tip_advice_en": "A 4-part response-letter frame: (1) a cover paragraph thanking the editor + reviewers; (2) a Reviewer 1 block — quote, response, change; (3) a Reviewer 2 block — same; (4) a closing paragraph of hope. Comment-handling tip — classify first: (a) ACCEPT — agree, can implement → 'Done, see page X'; (b) PARTIAL ACCEPT — agree in principle, can't fully implement → 'I have addressed this by Y, but full implementation requires Z, beyond the current scope'; (c) RESPECTFUL DISAGREE — you think the reviewer is wrong → respond with evidence; (d) CLARIFICATION — the reviewer misunderstood → explain and revise the wording in the paper to be clearer. Disagreement-response tip: NEVER 'the reviewer is wrong'. ALWAYS 'In the context of this study, X' / 'A different interpretation may be possible'. Numbers tip: with 12 comments, a 3–5 page response letter is about right. Too short = perfunctory; too long = the editor dislikes it. When resubmitting: subject line '[Manuscript ID] Revised submission with response to reviewers'. The email to the editor attaches: (a) a clean revised manuscript; (b) a tracked-changes revised manuscript; (c) the response letter PDF. Finally: peer review is an academic dialogue — not combat. Reviewers try to make the paper better (even when the tone is harsh). Thank them sincerely. Career-long: you will be someone's reviewer — practice empathy from this side.",
